# Arbiter ruling — fleet-task CLI, nQ round 1 of 3

arbiter: arbiter-cli-r1 (fresh; wrote no code, no tests)
suite: cd ~/agent-core/primitives/tools/fleet-task && bun test fleet-task.test.ts
tester report: 7 pass / 5 fail (arbiter re-run: 8 pass / 4 fail — see AC11)
verdict summary: ALL FIVE = BAD TEST. Route: test-maker. Coder has nothing to fix.

Evidence method: read of brief/design/test/impl + read-only CLI reproductions in
throwaway FLEET_TASKS_HOME dirs (no product/test files touched, nothing committed).

---

## AC1 — BAD TEST → test-maker

Failure: after `unit open`, test expects `mission.progress` all-zeros
(fleet-task.test.ts:94-100).

Rationale: the test misapplies the "Empty children → zeros" clause
(make brief line 111) to a mission that HAS one child — after `unit open` the
mission's children = [u-ac1] with rollup `pending`. Design §1.5
("units → mission: same aggregation over unit rollup_status values",
research/fleet-task-tool-design.md:108) and the brief's "From child statuses
(tasks→unit, units→mission)" (line 106) yield {pending:1, total:1}, which is
exactly what the impl computes (fleet-task.ts:86-92 → rollup.ts:31-45).
The empty-children rule governs a mission with ZERO units, not a mission whose
unit is empty. Impl is spec-faithful; the assertion mis-reads the spec.

## AC2 — BAD TEST → test-maker (two test-side bugs)

Failure: `unit.progress` is `undefined` (fleet-task.test.ts:177).

Rationale (a) — the actual failure: lines 172-176 place `["u-ac2"]` INSIDE the
`as Record<string, Record<string, unknown>>` type argument (a TypeScript
indexed-access type), not after the cast as a runtime index. At runtime `unit`
is the whole units MAP (`{u-ac2: {...}}`), which has no `progress` key — hence
`undefined`. Compare the correct parenthesization the same file uses at
lines 358-363 (AC7, passing). Arbiter reproduced the exact CLI sequence
manually: state.json on disk carries the correct
`unit.progress = {pending:1, in_progress:1, total:2}`.

Rationale (b) — latent, will fail next round if only (a) is fixed: the
mission.progress expectation at lines 188-194 is task-grain
{pending:1, in_progress:1, total:2}. Per design §1.5 the mission aggregates
UNIT rollup_status values, so with one unit at in_progress the correct value is
{in_progress:1, total:1} — what the impl stores (verified on disk).

## AC9 — BAD TEST → test-maker

Failure: rollup-changing write exited 1 (fleet-task.test.ts:429).

Rationale: the test patches the task `pending → completed` directly
(fleet-task.test.ts:422-428). The brief's pinned Legal transitions
(lines 119-122: `pending → in_progress → completed|cancelled`; also
`pending → cancelled`; all others reject) make `pending → completed` illegal.
The impl correctly rejects it (validate.ts:3-8, types.ts:64-69); arbiter
reproduced: exit 1, stderr "fleet-task: illegal transition for task only:
pending → completed". With the legal path (pending → in_progress → completed)
the impl exits 0 and posts contract-exact findings — verified live on
~/.tower/board.jsonl: topic `agent-core/fleet-tasks`, from
`fleet-task@agent-core`, body `u-arb9 pending→in_progress` etc. (tower.ts:12-35).
Fix: transition through in_progress before completing.

## AC10 — BAD TEST → test-maker

Failure: write exited 0 but stderr empty; expected Tower-failure warning
(fleet-task.test.ts:487).

Rationale: the test's ONLY write seeds a `pending` task into a unit whose
rollup is already `pending` (empty children → pending, brief line 111). No
rollup_status CHANGES, so per the D4 contract (brief lines 158-160: post only
"when a unit or mission rollup_status CHANGES") no Tower post is attempted and
stderr is correctly empty. AC10 demands "a rollup-changing write"; this write
is not one. Arbiter verified the impl path the AC actually specifies: a
rollup-changing write (pending → in_progress) with project_root under /tmp
exits 0 with stderr "fleet-task: warning: Tower post failed (post refused:
run from a real repo cwd, not scratch/temp)" (tower.ts:31-34; Tower refusal
regex ~/.tower/cli.mjs:124 covers /private/var/folders after realpath).
Fix: make the write flip the rollup (e.g. seed in_progress, or patch
pending → in_progress).

## AC11 — BAD TEST → test-maker

Failure: both writers exit 0 but `sawLock` false (fleet-task.test.ts:522);
tester run failed, arbiter re-run PASSED — flaky by construction.

Rationale: the test polls `existsSync(state.json.lock)` every 2 ms
(fleet-task.test.ts:513-516) for a lockfile whose critical section here is
sub-millisecond-to-few-ms (these writes cause no rollup change → no Tower
subprocess inside the lock). Observing an ephemeral file by interval polling
from a second process is a coin flip. The impl DOES implement the pinned
lockfile contract (store.ts:55-86: `wx` exclusive create, 30 s staleness
reclamation, 5 s bounded wait, unlock in finally) and the no-lost-updates /
valid-JSON legs of the AC pass. Fix: make the lock observable
deterministically — e.g. pre-create/hold the lockfile and assert the writer
waits then proceeds (or times out bounded), or drop `sawLock` and keep the
corruption/lost-update assertions which are the AC's substance.

